use std
into fit.amni

Imports: gfx.codec_jpeg_enc, os, gfx, math, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.codec_png, gfx.motion, gfx.scene3d and json.

This program fits a character model to target pictures (OpenMOSS renders on a plain light studio background) taken from one or more directions around it, and measures the match: it fits a camera per view to the target's silhouette, then the limbs, the outline shape, the lights and the textures, rendering the character over each target's background, and reports silhouette overlap, structural similarity and color difference per view, with side-by-side pictures and difference heat maps.

Module state: `var tw = 0`, `var th = 0` (target size, the same for every view), `var nview = 0`, `var cur = 0` (the view in use), per view `var v_px byte[>..]` (all targets' RGBA, view k at `k * tw * th * 4`), `var v_mask f32[>..]` (view k at `k * tw * th`), `var v_bg float3[>..]`, `var pcell = 4` (paint cell size in texels), `var v_fix bool[>..]`, `var v_yaw f32[>..]` (radians: 0 sees the character's front, positive turns the camera to the character's left side), `var v_d f32[>..]`, `var v_cy f32[>..]`, `var v_cx f32[>..]` (camera distance, height and sideways offset), grids for `paint` kept across views: `var g_off int[>..]`, `var g_w int[>..]`, `var g_r f32[>..]`, `var g_g f32[>..]`, `var g_b f32[>..]`, `var g_c int[>..]`, the shape controls kept across views: `var c_p float3[>..]`, `var c_m float3[>..]`, `var tpx byte[>..]` (target RGBA), `var tmask f32[>..]` (target coverage 0 or 1 per pixel), `var rpx byte[>..]` (render RGBA at target size, composited over the background), `var rmask f32[>..]` (render coverage per pixel, 0..1), `var bg = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }` (the target background in 0..255 per channel), `var cam_eye = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }`, `var cam_look = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }` (the fitted camera), `var hit_img int[>..]`, `var hit_u f32[>..]`, `var hit_v f32[>..]`, `var zb f32[>..]`, `var pal gfx::mat4[>..]`, `var fast = false`, `var shape_changed = false`, `var vx f32[>..]`, `var vy f32[>..]`, `var vz f32[>..]`, `var vw f32[>..]`, `var vis bool[>..]`, `var vrot linalg::Quat[>..]`, `var sm f32[>..]` (render once instead of three times while searching), `var pose_n int[>..]` (nodes the pose fit turned), `var pose_a f32[>..]` (their angles), `var img_changed int[>..]`, `var key_k = 1.0`, `var fill_k = 1.0`, `var amb_k = 1.0`, `var sun_az = 0.0`, `var sun_el = 0.0` (light fit factors), `var clear_c = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }`, `var item = -1`, `var asset = -1`, `var big = gfx::Texture {}` and `var fov = 0.5`.

`num(s const byte[:]) f32` parses one argument or token as a real and returns it `as f32`.

`at(px const byte[:], i int, c int) f32` is `px[i * 4 + c] as f32`.

`gray_bg()` finds the target background and its mask. The background is the per-channel median of the pixels on the picture's border rows and columns (collect them in three f32 lists, sort each with the standard sort, take the middle element) stored in `bg`. Diffusion pictures carry per-pixel grain, so the fill compares colors of a smoothed copy: the mean of each channel over the 5 by 5 window around the pixel (clamped at the edges), computed once into a float3 list. Then flood fill from every border pixel (an int stack of pixel indices and a byte list `seen` of tw * th): a pixel joins the background when, in the smoothed copy, its largest channel difference from the pixel it was reached from is at most 4, its largest channel difference from `bg` is at most 48, and its saturation (largest channel minus smallest) is at most 20. `tmask[i]` is 0 for background pixels and 1 for every other pixel.

`right_of(k int) float3` is `float3 { cos(v_yaw[k]), 0.0, -sin(v_yaw[k]) }` and `fwd_of(k int) float3` is `float3 { sin(v_yaw[k]), 0.0, cos(v_yaw[k]) }`.

`use_view(k int)` makes view k current: `cur = k`; copy its target RGBA into `tpx` and its mask into `tmask`, set `bg = v_bg[k]`, `cam_look = right_of(k) * v_cx[k] + float3 { 0.0, v_cy[k], 0.0 }` and `cam_eye = cam_look + fwd_of(k) * v_d[k]`. Finally call `set_lights()` (the rig follows the view's camera).

`cam_fit(k int, iters int) bool` fits view k's camera to its silhouette (a fixed view, `v_fix[k]`, keeps its camera: `use_view(k)` and return true): `use_view(k)`; with `ty0, ty1, tcx = bbox(tmask)` (false when ty1 <= ty0); `iters` times: `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)`, `my0, my1, mcx = bbox(rmask)` (false when my1 <= my0); `wpp = 2 v_d[k] tan(fov / 2) / th`; `v_cy[k] -= (((my0 + my1) as f32 - (ty0 + ty1) as f32) / 2) * wpp`; `v_cx[k] += (mcx - tcx) * wpp`; `v_d[k] *= (my1 - my0) as f32 / (ty1 - ty0) as f32`; `use_view(k)`. True at the end.

`load_pose(path const byte[:])` reads a pose file with the unqualified `read_file(path, data)` into a `byte[>..]` (return when it fails). Each non-empty line is a node name followed by ten reals separated by single spaces: translation x y z, rotation quaternion x y z w, scale x y z. For each line find the node with `gltf::find_node(asset, name)` and, when it is not negative, call `s3d::set_node(item, node, float3 { tx, ty, tz }, linalg::Quat { qx, qy, qz, qw }, float3 { sx, sy, sz })`.

`shot(eye float3, look float3)` renders the character at twice the target size and downsamples it: set `s3d::view` to an `s3d::Camera` from eye to look, up (0, 1, 0), fov_y `fov`, near 0.05, far 100; call `s3d::render(big)` once when `fast`, else three times; `px = gfx::read_pixels(big)`. A big pixel is empty when each of its red, green and blue differs by at most 1 from `clear_c` (the flat color Helium writes where nothing is drawn while sky drawing is off). Fill `rmask` and `rpx` for each target pixel (x, y) from the four big pixels (2x + u, 2y + v) for u, v in 0..1: coverage is the share of non-empty ones (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 or 1); each output channel c is the mean over the four of (the big pixel's channel when it is non-empty, else `bg` channel c), rounded and clamped to 0..255; alpha 255.

`bbox(m const f32[:]) int, int, f32` returns the first row with any pixel of m at least 0.5, the last such row, and the mean column of those pixels (as f32); returns 0, 0, 0 when there are none.

`lab(r f32, g f32, b f32) float3` converts an sRGB color in 0..255 to CIE L*a*b* (D65): linearize each channel `c / 255` with the sRGB curve, convert to XYZ with the standard sRGB matrix, divide by the white point (0.95047, 1.0, 1.08883), apply `f(t) = t^(1/3)` when t > 0.008856 else `7.787 t + 16 / 116`, then L = 116 fy - 16, a = 500 (fx - fy), b = 200 (fy - fz).

`de2000(p float3, q float3) f32` is the CIEDE2000 color difference between two Lab colors with kL = kC = kH = 1, following Sharma, Wu and Dalal (2005) exactly: C* means, the G factor with 25^7, a' scaling, C' and h' (degrees in 0..360, 0 when a' and b' are both 0), ΔL', ΔC', Δh' with the ±180 wrap, ΔH', the means L', C' and h' with the hue-mean rule, T, Δθ, R_C, S_L, S_C, S_H, R_T, and the square root of the sum.

`metrics(heat byte[>..]&) f32, f32, f32, f32` compares `tpx`/`tmask` with `rpx`/`rmask` and fills `heat` (tw * th * 4 bytes). IoU: intersection is the sum of `min(tmask, rmask)`, union the sum of `max(tmask, rmask)`. Color: over the pixels where `max(tmask[i], rmask[i]) > 0` take `d = de2000(lab of the target pixel, lab of the render pixel)`, sum it and count; the heat pixel is black when d is 0 rising to red at d = 10 and to white at d = 20 and above (red = min(d / 10, 1) * 255, green and blue = clamp((d - 10) / 10, 0, 1) * 255), and for the other pixels the heat pixel is the target pixel at a quarter brightness. Structural similarity on luminance `0.299 r + 0.587 g + 0.114 b` over the union mask's bounding box (rows and columns where the union is above 0): for every window of 7 by 7 pixels whose top-left corner steps by 2 in each direction inside that box, compute the means, variances and covariance of target and render luminance and `((2 mx my + C1)(2 cxy + C2)) / ((mx² + my² + C1)(vx + vy + C2))` with C1 = (0.01 · 255)² and C2 = (0.03 · 255)², and average those windows that hold at least one union pixel. Return IoU, SSIM, mean ΔE (0 when no pixels counted) and the share of counted pixels with ΔE below 3.

`project()` computes, for every vertex of every primitive of the asset (global vertex index g = `prim.vfirst + v`; the lists are sized `gltf::verts.len / gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`), its pixel position `vx[g]`, `vy[g]`, depth `vz[g]`, clip w `vw[g]`, its world position in `vpw` (f32, three per vertex at 3g; a module var sized with the others), and the world-to-bind rotation `vrot[g]`, using the final fitted camera and the current pose. Call `s3d::pose(item)` first. `vp = gfx::perspective(fov, tw as f32 / th as f32, 0.05, 100.0) * gfx::look_at(cam_eye, cam_look, float3 { 0.0, 1.0, 0.0 })`. For every node n of the asset (from `gltf::assets[asset].nodes.first`, count `.nodes.count`) with `gltf::nodes[n].mesh >= 0`: when its `skin >= 0` fill `pal` with `motion::joints(gltf::nodes[n].skin, pal)`. For each vertex of each of its primitives (floats at `g * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`), its world position is, when the node is skinned, the sum over all four joints c = 0, 1, 2 and 3 (four terms) of `weight[22 + c] * linalg::point(pal[joint[18 + c] as int], position)` and `vrot` the conjugate of `mquat(pal[joint with the largest weight])`, else `linalg::point(motion::world[n], position)` and `vrot` the conjugate of `mquat(motion::world[n])`; project it with vp to clip space (x, y, z, w); store `vw = w` and, when w > 0, `vx = (x / w * 0.5 + 0.5) * tw`, `vy = (0.5 - y / w * 0.5) * th`, `vz = z / w`.

`raster()` finds, for each target pixel, which texture spot of the character it shows. Call `project()`. Clear `hit_img` (int, tw * th, -1), `hit_u`, `hit_v`, `hit_f` (f32, tw * th; `hit_f` is a module var) and a depth list `zb` (f32, tw * th, 1e30). For every primitive of every mesh node of the asset whose material has a base texture (`m = gltf::materials[prim.material]`, `m.base_tex.texture >= 0`, `img = gltf::textures[m.base_tex.texture].image >= 0`), rasterize each triangle (indices `gltf::indices[prim.ifirst + 3 t + k]`, local to the primitive, so the global vertex is `prim.vfirst + index`; skip it when any vertex has `vw <= 0`) over its pixel bounding box clamped to the picture, testing pixel centers with edge functions and barycentric weights in both windings; where the interpolated depth is below `zb`, interpolate uv0 (floats 6, 7), and when the base texture's alpha at that uv (see `texel`) is at least 128 store depth, `img`, u, v and the triangle's facing in `hit_f`: with world positions p0, p1, p2 from `vpw`, `abs(dot(normalize(cross(p1 - p0, p2 - p0)), normalize(cam_eye - (p0 + p1 + p2) / 3)))` (0 when the cross product is shorter than 1e-12). Also rasterize primitives without a base texture into `zb` only (depth, no hit), so they hide what is behind them.

`texel(img int, u f32, v f32) int` is the byte offset into `gltf::pixels` of the texel at uv (u, v) of image img: `im = gltf::images[img]`, `x = clamp(floor(fract(u) * im.width), 0, im.width - 1)`, `y = clamp(floor(fract(v) * im.height), 0, im.height - 1)`, offset `im.pixels.first + (y * im.width + x) * 4`.

`lin(c f32) f32` turns an sRGB channel in 0..255 into linear 0..1 and `enc(l f32) f32` turns linear 0..1 back into 0..255 (both with the exact sRGB curve).

`pattern_fit()` lines up patterned textures (plaid, stripes, print) with the targets before painting, because painting multiplies texels and cannot move a pattern. Candidates are the base-color images used by at least one material (`gltf::materials[m].base_tex.texture >= 0` and its texture's image `img >= 0`) whose pixels (`gltf::image_pixels(img)`, RGBA) have a luminance standard deviation above 0.08 (0..1 units, sampled every 7th texel). For each candidate img: gather, over all views, the pixels showing it: for each view k `use_view(k)`, `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)`, `raster()`, and keep the pixel indices i with `hit_img[i] == img`, `tmask[i] == 1` and `rmask[i] == 1` per view (a list of (view, pixel) pairs); skip the image when fewer than 2000 pixels in total. Its cost is the mean CIEDE2000 (`de2000(lab(target), lab(render))`) over those pairs after a fresh `shot` of each involved view. The transform is applied to every material using img: `base_tex.o = float2 { du, dv }` and `base_tex.m = float4 { 1.0 + ds, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 + ds }` (offset du, dv, uniform scale 1 + ds). Coordinate search from (0, 0, 0): three passes with steps (0.02, 0.02, 0.02), then (0.007, 0.007, 0.007), then (0.002, 0.002, 0.002) for du, dv, ds; in each pass try each parameter plus and minus its step and keep a change only when the cost drops, repeating a parameter while it keeps improving (at most 7 times); limits |du| and |dv| at most 0.15, |ds| at most 0.1. Print "pattern " img " du " du " dv " dv " ds " ds " de " before " -> " after (4 decimals). When the best is not (0, 0, 0), bake it: resample the image into a copy so that the texel at (x, y) takes the bilinear sample of the old image at uv `((x + 0.5) / w * (1 + ds) + du, (y + 0.5) / h * (1 + ds) + dv)` wrapped with fract (the same mapping the shader applies: uv' = m · uv + o), write the copy back into the image's pixels, set `img_changed[img] = 1`, re-upload (`gfx::update_texture(s3d::tex_srgb[img], gltf::image_pixels(img))` and `gfx::generate_mips` when `s3d::tex_srgb[img].id != 0`), and restore every material's base_tex transform to identity (m = (1, 0, 0, 1), o = (0, 0)).

`view_gain()` makes the extra full-body views agree with the first view's exposure: each MOSS image comes with its own lighting, so only view 0 and fixed views set absolute color. For each view k > 0 with `!v_fix[k]`: `use_view(k)`, `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)`, then over pixels with `tmask[i] == 1` and `rmask[i] == 1` sum the linear target and the linear render per channel; when at least 2000 pixels count, the gain per channel is `clamp(render sum / target sum, 0.5, 2.0)`; multiply every linear target channel of the view (all pixels, in `v_px` at the view's offset) by it and store it back encoded (keep alpha). Print "gain " k " " the three gains (4 decimals).

`material_fit()` matches each textured material's sheen and surface depth, which painting (base color only) cannot: for each material m with a base texture whose image `img` has at least 2000 (view, pixel) pairs (gathered as in `pattern_fit`: `hit_img[i] == img` with both masks set, over all views after `shot` and `raster`), search its `roughness` and `normal_scale` (factors on the starting values `r0` and `n0`: roughness `clamp(r0 * fr, 0.04, 1.0)`, normal scale `n0 * fn`, with fr in 0.7..1.4, so a material stays near its authored class and a knit or cotton cannot turn glossy to brighten itself, and fn in 0..2) by coordinate search on the mean CIEDE2000 over those pairs (fresh `shot` of each involved view per candidate): two passes with multiplicative steps 1.3 then 1.1 for fr and additive steps 0.3 then 0.1 for fn, each parameter tried up and down and repeated while it improves (at most 7 times). Materials sharing the image change together. Print "material " img " rough " r " nscale " n " de " before " -> " after (4 decimals). The changed factors stay in `gltf::materials`; set `mat_changed = true` (a module var, false at first) when any material changed.

`paint_gather()` adds the current view's corrections to the paint grids. Call `raster()` (for the current view's camera, `cam_eye`/`cam_look`, and the latest `shot`). For each image that has hits there is one correction grid of `ceil(width / pcell)` by `ceil(height / pcell)` cells with three f32 sums and a count (allocated on first use in `g_r`, `g_g`, `g_b`, `g_c` at offset `g_off[img]` with width `g_w[img]`). For every pixel i with `tmask[i] == 1`, `rmask[i] == 1` and `hit_img[i] >= 0`: for each channel the ratio is `clamp((lin(target) + 0.004) / (lin(render) + 0.004), 0.25, 4.0)`; when the view does not set absolute color (`cur != 0 && !v_fix[cur]`), keep only its fine detail, because its photo has its own lighting: before the pixel loop, build per-channel ratio maps over the pixels that pass every skip test below (ratio as above, after the hue taming), take their box mean over the 31 by 31 neighborhood of each pixel counting only those pixels (integral images of the ratio sums and of the counts, f64), and use `clamp(ratio / mean, 0.5, 2.0)` per channel instead of the ratio (pixels whose neighborhood has fewer than 50 valid pixels are skipped); skip pixels whose `hit_f[i]` is below 0.5 (surfaces seen edge-on get smeared colors from neighbors) and add each remaining pixel's three ratios and its count weighted by `pow(hit_f[i], 7) * (if v_fix[cur] { 4 } else { 1 })` (the view that sees a spot most squarely, and the detailed close-ups, win where views overlap instead of being averaged; the grids divide sums by counts, so the weights stay consistent; integer counts use `max(1, round(weight * 16))`); skip pixels whose hit texel's alpha (the texel at the pixel's uv in `gltf::pixels`, byte 3) is below 230 (hair-card edges and stray strands cross the face, and painting them with skin makes teal streaks); in every pass tame the hue: with `lr` the luminance ratio `clamp((Y(target) + 0.004) / (Y(render) + 0.004), 0.25, 4.0)` (Y = 0.2126 r + 0.7152 g + 0.0722 b of the linear colors), each channel's ratio becomes `lr * sqrt(clamp(ratio / lr, 0.5, 2.0))`; in every pass skip pixels near a part boundary (belt over skirt or sock into shoe sits a few pixels apart in the target and the render, and painting across it mixes the two materials): the pixel is skipped when any pixel within 2 pixels of it (the 5 by 5 block, clipped to the picture) has a different `hit_img` (including -1); when `pcell == 1` (the fine pass) also skip pixels on strong target edges (the sum of the absolute differences of the target's linear luminance between the pixels left and right of it and between the pixels above and below it is above 0.15; pixels at the image border are skipped) and clamp `lr` to 0.5..2 there instead of 0.25..4 (a small misalignment at a lip line or nostril must not swing the hue), added to the image's cell at `(floor(fract(u) * width / pcell), floor(fract(v) * height / pcell))`.

`paint(fine bool) int` corrects the character's base textures so every view's render moves toward its target (with `fine`, only the fixed close-up views take part). Clear the grids (`g_off` and `g_w` sized to `gltf::images.len` with -1 and 0, the sum lists emptied) and `img_changed` (sized to `gltf::images.len`, 0). Gather in two passes over the views (when `fine`, only those with `v_fix[k]`): first the views that set absolute color (k == 0 or `v_fix[k]`), then the others; for each, `use_view(k)`, `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)`, `paint_gather()`. A per-cell flag list `g_abs` (byte, parallel to the counts, 0 at first, grown with the grids) marks cells an absolute view added to; a view that does not set absolute color adds only to cells whose `g_abs` is 0 (it fills in what the front and close-ups cannot see, and never dilutes their corrections). Then feather each image's grid so corrections fade out instead of stopping at a hard edge: per cell keep a log ratio per channel (`ln(mean ratio)` for cells with a count, else 0) and a strength (1 for cells with a count, else 0); 7 times, every cell without a count whose 4-neighbors (inside the grid) include at least one with strength above 0 takes, per channel, the strength-weighted mean of those neighbors' log ratios and strength `0.7 * ` the mean of their strengths (compute each round from the previous round's values, in a copy). Then for every cell with strength above 0.01, and every texel of that pcell by pcell block, multiply each linear texel channel by `exp(0.8 * strength * log ratio)` (for cells with a count this is `pow(mean ratio, 0.8)`), clamp to 0..1 and store it back encoded into `gltf::pixels` (keep alpha), and set `img_changed[img] = 1`. Re-upload every changed image: `gfx::update_texture(s3d::tex_srgb[img], gltf::image_pixels(img))` and `gfx::generate_mips(s3d::tex_srgb[img])` when `s3d::tex_srgb[img].id != 0`. Return the number of cells changed.

`turn(node int, ang f32)` turns one joint about the camera's viewing axis (world z) by `ang` radians on top of its rest pose: with `n = gltf::nodes[node]`, `p = n.parent`, the axis in the parent's frame is `linalg::unit3(linalg::qrotate(qconj(mquat(motion::world[p])), float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 }))` when p >= 0 (else world z), and the joint is set with `s3d::set_node(item, node, n.t, linalg::qnorm(linalg::qmul(linalg::qaxis(axis, ang), n.r)), n.s)`. Record it in `pose_n`/`pose_a` (replace the angle when the node is already listed). `mquat(m gfx::mat4) linalg::Quat` is the rotation of a matrix whose columns may be scaled (normalize the three columns, then the standard matrix-to-quaternion conversion) and `qconj(q)` the conjugate.

`score() f32` is the mean over all views k of: `use_view(k)`, `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)` and the silhouette IoU (intersection over union of `tmask` and `rmask`, as in `metrics`).

`pose_fit()` matches the limbs to the target silhouette by coordinate search. `fast = true`. Joints, in order: upperarm_l, upperarm_r, lowerarm_l, lowerarm_r, thigh_l, thigh_r, calf_l, calf_r (skip names `gltf::find_node` cannot find). Call `s3d::pose(item)` once so `motion::world` is current. Two passes; in each pass, for each joint with current angle `a0` (0 at first) and best score `best = score()`: for the step sizes 0.28, 0.14, 0.07, 0.035 and 0.014 radians, try `a0 + step` and `a0 - step` with `turn`; keep whichever raises the score (update `a0` and `best`), else restore `turn(node, a0)`. `fast = false`. Print "pose " plus each joint's name and angle (4 decimals).

`save_pose(path const byte[:])` writes the turned joints as a pose file in the same format `load_pose` reads (name, translation, rotation, scale of the override as set by `turn`, one line each) with the unqualified `write_file`.

`shape_gather()` adds the current view's outline moves to the shape controls. Call `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)` and `raster()`. A vertex is visible when `vw > 0`, its pixel is inside the picture and `vz` is at most `zb` at its pixel plus 0.0005. Smooth `rmask` with a 5 by 5 box into `sm`. For every visible vertex whose pixel touches the render outline (`rmask` at its pixel at least 0.5 and some pixel within 2 pixels below 0.5): the outward normal (nx, ny) in the picture is `-(gradient of sm)` by central differences, normalized (skip when the gradient length is below 0.01). March from the vertex pixel along the normal in 1-pixel steps up to 40: when `tmask` there is 1, march outward until it turns 0 and the offset is the steps taken minus 0.5; else march inward until `tmask` is 1 and the offset is minus the steps plus 0.5; skip when the edge is not found. With `wpp = 2 v_d[cur] tan(fov / 2) / th`, the world move is `(right_of(cur) * nx - float3 { 0.0, 1.0, 0.0 } * ny) * (0.7 * offset * wpp)` (picture y points down). Append a control point: bind-space position (the vertex's position floats 0..2) to `c_p` and bind-space move `linalg::qrotate(vrot, world move)` to `c_m`.

`shape_fit(round int) int` moves the character's outline toward every view's target outline. Clear `c_p` and `c_m`; for each view k that is not fixed (`!v_fix[k]`: a close-up's frame cuts the body, so its outline is not the character's): `use_view(k)` and `shape_gather()`. Then every vertex of every primitive of the asset moves by the normalized Gaussian blend of the control moves: `w = exp(-r² / (2 σ²))` with r the bind-space distance and σ = 0.035, `move = Σ w m / max(Σ w, 1)` (loop over controls; skip controls farther than 3σ). Add the move to position floats 0..2 and set `s3d::uploaded_verts = -2` so the geometry is uploaded again. Set `shape_changed = true`. Return the number of control points.

`set_lights()` applies the light fit factors in a studio rig that turns with the camera (each target photo is lit from its own camera's side): with `ca = atan2(cam_eye.x - cam_look.x, cam_eye.z - cam_look.z)` and `rot(x, y, z) = float3 { x * cos(ca) + z * sin(ca), y, -x * sin(ca) + z * cos(ca) }` (f32 math), `s3d::sun = s3d::Sun { to_sun: linalg::unit3(rot(0.35 + sun_az, 0.8 + sun_el, 1.0)), rgb: float3 { 2.2 * key_k, 2.2 * key_k, 2.2 * key_k }, shadows: true, shadow_far: 10.0, map_size: 2048 }`; `s3d::lights` cleared and `s3d::add_light(rot(-14.0, 7.0, 14.0), float3 { 260.0 * fill_k, 260.0 * fill_k, 260.0 * fill_k }, 1000.0)`; `s3d::sky.ambient = amb_k`.

`tone() f32` is the mean over the views that set absolute color (k == 0 or `v_fix[k]`; the extra full-body views get their exposure from `view_gain` instead) of: `use_view(k)`, `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)` and the mean CIEDE2000 difference over pixels where both `tmask` and `rmask` are 1 (1000 for a view with none).

`light_fit()` lowers that difference by coordinate search: `fast = true`; three passes over the factors key_k, fill_k, amb_k (multiplied or divided by 1.25 then 1.1) and sun_az, sun_el (plus or minus 0.2 then 0.07): try each change with `set_lights()` and `tone()`, keep it when the difference drops, else undo it. `fast = false`; call `set_lights()`. Print "light key " key_k " fill " fill_k " amb " amb_k " az " sun_az " el " sun_el (4 decimals).

`emit(d json::Doc&, n int, out byte[>..]&)` writes JSON node n back as compact JSON text using the json module's formatters (`json::format_key`, `json::format_string`, `json::format_int`, `json::format_number`, `json::format_bool`, `json::format_null`): objects with their members in order, arrays with their elements, strings, whole numbers as ints, other numbers as numbers.

`export_glb(src const byte[:], out const byte[:], prefix const byte[:])` writes a copy of the source .glb whose changed images are the painted ones. Read src with `read_file`; the JSON chunk is at byte 20 with length `u32le(12)`, the binary chunk follows (its length at the 4 bytes after the JSON chunk, its data 8 bytes later). Parse the JSON with `json::parse`. The changed images' old bytes are dropped: call a JSON bufferView dead when only changed images reference it (no accessor and no unchanged image uses it). New binary: for each JSON bufferView in index order, when it is dead it becomes `{"buffer":0,"byteOffset":0,"byteLength":4}` and copies nothing; otherwise copy its bytes (its byteOffset, 0 when absent, and byteLength into the old binary) to the end of the new binary padded with zeros to a multiple of 4, and record its new byteOffset (the other members of the view stay as they were). The new binary starts with 4 zero bytes so dead views have something to point at. Then for each image k of the asset (global image `gltf::assets[asset].images.first + k`) with `img_changed` set, its new bytes padded with zeros to a multiple of 4, remembering offset and length: when the source image's mimeType is "image/jpeg", the JPEG made with `codec_jpeg_enc::encode(gltf::image_pixels(img), width, height, 92, bytes)` and mime "image/jpeg"; otherwise the bytes of the file `prefix + "_tex" + global index + ".png"` (already saved) and mime "image/png". New JSON: emit the root object member by member (walk an object's members with `json::each(d, n) { c => ... }`, naming each with `json::name_of(d, c)`, or look one up with `json::member(d, n, key)`; `json::element` only indexes arrays and gives -1 on an object, so never use it for members; the same holds inside materials); for "bufferViews" emit the old elements with their new byteOffset (dead ones as described) and then one `{"buffer":0,"byteOffset":o,"byteLength":l}` per new image; for "images" emit each element as is except changed ones, which become `{"bufferView":v,"mimeType":<its new mime>,"name":<old name or "">}` with v the new view's index; when `shape_changed`, also overwrite vertex positions in the new binary: for each mesh k of the JSON "meshes" array (the asset's mesh `gltf::assets[asset].meshes.first + k`) and each of its primitives j (`gltf::prims[mesh.prims.first + j]`), take the accessor `attributes.POSITION`, its bufferView's byteOffset (0 when absent) plus the accessor's byteOffset (0 when absent), the stride (the view's byteStride, else 12) and its count, and write each vertex v's three position floats from `gltf::verts[(prim.vfirst + v) * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS]` as little-endian f32 at that offset plus v times the stride (only when the count equals `prim.vcount`); also set that accessor's "min" and "max" to the new bounds when emitting "accessors". for "materials" emit each material k (the asset's material `gltf::assets[asset].materials.first + k`) as is except that, when `mat_changed`, its `pbrMetallicRoughness.roughnessFactor` is written as the material's current `roughness` and, when it has a `normalTexture`, that object's `scale` as its current `normal_scale` (emit the objects member by member, replacing just those numbers or adding them when absent); for "buffers" emit `[{"byteLength":<new binary length>}]`; everything else as is. Pad the JSON with spaces to a multiple of 4. Write "glTF", version 2, total length, the JSON chunk (length, "JSON") and the binary chunk (length, "BIN" and a zero byte), all little-endian, with `write_file`. Print "glb " plus the path.

`report(tag const byte[:], save bool, prefix const byte[:])` measures every view: for each view k, `use_view(k)`, `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)`, `iou, ssim, de, good = metrics(heat)`, and print "<tag> v<k> iou " iou " ssim " ssim " de " de " good " good " cam " v_cx[k] " " v_cy[k] " " v_d[k], each number with 4 decimals; then print "<tag> mean iou .. ssim .. de .. good .." over the views. When `save`, also save three pictures per view with `gfx::save_shot` (each a `gfx::Shot { w, h, pf: 376840196, px }`): `prefix + "_v" + k + "_render.png"` (rpx), `prefix + "_v" + k + "_heat.png"` (heat) and `prefix + "_v" + k + "_side.png"`, which is target, render and heat side by side (3 tw wide, th tall).

Environment variables are read from `/proc/self/environ` with the unqualified `read_file` (NUL-separated NAME=value entries; there is no getenv): `env_get(name, out) bool` copies the value and returns whether it is set. A stage switch is on unless its variable is set to "0" (`env_get` and compare the value); `FIT_MASK` is on when set at all. Never declare extern getenv or strcmp: the native backend has neither.

`main()`: with `a = args()`, `a[1]` is the .glb, `a[2]` the targets, `a[3]` the output prefix, and `a[4]` an optional pose file. The targets are separated by commas, each a .png path optionally followed by colon-separated numbers: the view's yaw in degrees (0 when absent), then optionally its camera distance, height and sideways offset in meters, which make it a fixed view (a close-up whose frame cuts the body, so its camera is never fitted), at most 7. Read each with the unqualified `read_file` and decode it with `codec_png::decode(data, tpx)`; the first sets `tw`, `th`, later ones with another size are skipped with a message ("target failed" and skip when decoding fails). For each kept target: resize `tmask` to tw * th, call `gray_bg()`, append `tpx` to `v_px`, `tmask` to `v_mask`, `bg` to `v_bg`, the yaw in radians to `v_yaw`, and the given distance, height and offset (3.2, 0.85, 0 when absent) to `v_d`, `v_cy`, `v_cx`, and whether they were given to `v_fix`; count `nview`. Return with "no targets" when nview is 0. When the environment variable `FIT_MASK` is set (any value), save each view's mask as `a[3] + "_v" + k + "_mask.png"` (white for the character, black for background, RGBA with `gfx::save_shot`) without opening a device, print "mask v" k " " plus the share of character pixels (4 decimals), and return. Resize `rmask` to tw * th. Open headless with `gfx::open_headless(tw, th)` (on failure print "gpu: skipped (no device: " plus `gfx::error()` plus ")" and return). Load the glb with `gltf::load` into `asset` (negative: print "load failed: " plus `gltf::error()` and return), `item = s3d::add(asset)`, `s3d::place(item, gfx::mat4_identity)`, and when `a.len > 4` call `load_pose(a[4])`. Light it as a soft light-gray photo studio: `s3d::sky = s3d::Sky { zenith: float3 { 0.6, 0.6, 0.6 }, horizon: float3 { 0.55, 0.55, 0.55 }, ground: float3 { 0.3, 0.3, 0.3 }, ambient: 1.0, draw: false }`, `set_lights()`, `s3d::look.bloom = 0.0`, `s3d::look.desaturate = 0.0`, `s3d::look.curve = 1`. `s3d::init(2 * tw, 2 * th)` and `big = gfx::render_target(2 * tw, 2 * th, gfx::RGBA8)`. Learn the empty color: place the item at `gfx::translate(float3 { 0.0, -1000.0, 0.0 })`, set `s3d::view` to a camera from (0, 0.85, 3.2) to (0, 0.85, 0) with fov_y `fov`, render `big` three times, read its pixels and store the first pixel's red, green and blue in `clear_c`; print "clear " plus the three values; then place the item back at `gfx::mat4_identity`.
Fit every view's camera with `cam_fit(k, 7)` (print "empty view " k and return when it is false). Unless the environment variable `FIT_POSE` is "0": `pose_fit()`, refit every camera with `cam_fit(k, 3)` and `save_pose(a[3] + "_pose.txt")`. Only when `FIT_SHAPE` is "1" (opt-in: outline marching drags faces out of shape, and the fit scores better without it): 4 rounds of `shape_fit(r)`, refitting every camera with `cam_fit(k, 3)` after each, then `report("shape" + round, false, a[3])`. Unless `FIT_LIGHT` is "0", `light_fit()`. Then `report("fit", false, a[3])`. Unless `FIT_GAIN` is "0", `view_gain()`. Unless `FIT_MATERIAL` is "0", `material_fit()`. Unless `FIT_PATTERN` is "0", `pattern_fit()` (resize `img_changed` to `gltf::images.len` with 0 first, keeping earlier flags). Unless `FIT_PAINT` is "0": 4 rounds of `paint(false)` then `report("paint" + round, false, a[3])`; then, when any view is fixed, set `pcell = 1` and run 4 rounds of `paint(true)` then `report("fine" + round, false, a[3])`, and set `pcell = 4` again; afterwards save every image with `img_changed` set as `a[3] + "_tex" + index + ".png"` with `gfx::save_shot` (RGBA, width and height of the image, pixels from `gltf::image_pixels`). When any were saved or `shape_changed` or `mat_changed`, call `export_glb(a[1], a[3] + ".glb", a[3])`. Finally `report("final", true, a[3])`.
